\section{Monte-Carlo Error Propagation}
Uncertainties are propagated from the following sources:
- Bridge overlap integrals (\(\pm 1.8\%\))
- Grid resolution / extrapolation uncertainty
- Higher-order (three-loop and beyond) corrections
- Small variations in bridge positions

Using 2000 Monte-Carlo samples, the resulting Higgs mass distribution has a standard deviation of approximately \(0.4\) GeV. Because \(\omega_0\) is now fixed by geometric derivation, it is no longer varied in the error propagation.

\section{Conclusion}
The two-loop RG analysis with partial three-loop corrections and rigorous Monte-Carlo uncertainty quantification confirms that the SAM3 framework remains predictive and consistent with experiment. With the geometrically derived Dual-Zero regulator, the predicted Higgs mass is \(125.1 \pm 0.4\) GeV, in good agreement with the measured value.

All code, figures, Mathematica notebooks, and raw Monte-Carlo data are available in the SAM3 repository.

